---
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const resumeUrl = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_RESUME_URL;
const personSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: SITE.name,
  url: SITE.url,
  sameAs: [SITE.github, SITE.ferret],
  knowsAbout: ['Developer tools', 'Programming languages', 'Infrastructure', 'Automation'],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Timofei Voronov and a progression from reusable libraries and integration tooling to infrastructure, language, and runtime design."
  structuredData={personSchema}
>
  <div class="page-shell">
    <header class="page-header">
      <div>
        <p class="page-header__eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1>Tools are how I think about systems.</h1>
      </div>
      <p class="page-header__summary">
        I am a software engineer focused on developer tooling, programming languages,
        infrastructure, and carefully designed abstractions.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-layout">
      <aside class="prose-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="section-label">A short account</p>
        <a href="#progression">Progression</a>
        <a href="#working-style">Working style</a>
        <a href="#places">Places</a>
      </aside>
      <article class="prose">
        <h2 id="progression">A progression of interests</h2>
        <p>
          Much of my work has been around backend systems, infrastructure, integrations, and the
          tools people use to work with them. The path began with small reusable JavaScript and .NET
          utilities, moved through build systems, Electron IPC, connected devices, and Node-RED
          integrations, and later concentrated on Go infrastructure.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ferret brings many of those interests together. It is a declarative language and runtime
          for data automation, which makes language design, embedding, extensibility, browser
          control, and developer experience parts of the same system.
        </p>

        <h2 id="working-style">Working style</h2>
        <p>
          I like building tools that remove friction without hiding the underlying system. A useful
          abstraction should clarify ownership and failure modes, keep escape hatches visible, and
          remain small enough to understand.
        </p>
        <p>
          That interest appears at different scales here: a transaction helper, a logging
          integration, a set of visual-programming nodes, and a complete language runtime. The scale
          changes; the attention to the interface does not.
        </p>

        <h2 id="places">Places</h2>
        <p>
          I grew up in Vladivostok and have lived and worked across countries. Those details matter
          less here than the continuity of the work: building practical infrastructure, publishing
          reusable pieces, and learning what makes a tool hospitable to its users.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can follow the public work on <a href={SITE.github}>GitHub</a> or read more about{' '}
          <a href={SITE.ferret}>Ferret</a>.
        </p>
        {
          resumeUrl && (
            <p>
              <a class="text-link" href={resumeUrl}>
                Résumé
              </a>
            </p>
          )
        }
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
